Sval: report WebGPU timestamp-query as unsupported

A new RFPTarget, WebGPUTimestampQueryHidden, leaves timestamp-query out of
GPUAdapter.features, so a page cannot time GPU work precisely. requestDevice
then refuses it with the TypeError an adapter that lacks the feature gives,
which is what a page sees on hardware without it; the not-implemented path
would name the browser and a Bugzilla URL. Quantising the timings instead
needs a patch to vendored wgpu-core. The target is Doc-gated, so
granularOverrides gives a site the feature back.

No chrome API can change what Adapter lists.

diff --git a/dom/webgpu/Adapter.cpp b/dom/webgpu/Adapter.cpp
index 568509ad2b..1bebedff23 100644
--- a/dom/webgpu/Adapter.cpp
+++ b/dom/webgpu/Adapter.cpp
@@ -289,6 +289,11 @@ Adapter::Adapter(Instance* const aParent, WebGPUChild* const aChild,
     return ret;
   }();
 
+  // Under WebGPUTimestampQueryHidden the adapter reports timestamp-query as
+  // one that lacks it would, so requestDevice refuses it the same way.
+  const bool hideTimestampQuery = GetParentObject()->ShouldResistFingerprinting(
+      RFPTarget::WebGPUTimestampQueryHidden);
+
   auto remainingFeatureBits = aInfo->features;
   auto bitMask = decltype(remainingFeatureBits){0};
   while (remainingFeatureBits) {
@@ -305,6 +310,10 @@ Adapter::Adapter(Instance* const aParent, WebGPUChild* const aChild,
 
     const auto featureForBit = FEATURE_BY_BIT.find(bit);
     if (featureForBit != FEATURE_BY_BIT.end()) {
+      if (hideTimestampQuery &&
+          featureForBit->second == dom::GPUFeatureName::Timestamp_query) {
+        continue;
+      }
       mFeatures->Add(featureForBit->second, ignoredRv);
     } else {
       // One of two cases:
